# State-matched one-pass hashing scope v1

## Question

Can either of the two algebraically state-matched hash/SGD endpoints meet the
original quality/time gate, or is full sparse Ridge already the stronger simple
baseline on 20 Newsgroups?

## Fixed endpoints

1. `hash_16384_no_average`: `n_features=2**14`, `average=False`.
2. `hash_4096_average`: `n_features=2**12`, `average=True`.

All other vectorizer, SGD, batch, split, metadata, single-pass, and three-order
settings remain identical to `discriminative_streaming_hash_pilot_v1`.
Neither dimension was chosen from test performance: both are the largest nearby
powers of two implied by the full Ridge coefficient-state crossover under the
corresponding number of retained parameter arrays.

## Unchanged decision gate

An endpoint must have median macro-F1 loss at most 0.05, median wall ratio at
most 0.75, and measured NumPy parameter arrays no larger than the full Ridge
coefficient/intercept byte reference.  Do not relax these thresholds.

If neither passes, retire streaming hashing on this benchmark.  A later
streaming application must use a source-defined larger task where the full
sparse baseline actually creates a resource constraint; it must not infer a
benefit from 20 Newsgroups.  If one passes, it is only a strong simple baseline
for a prospective online-IDF/moment comparison, not a new result.

